---
import PowerFlowDemo from './PowerFlowDemo.astro';
---

<section class="section section--alt power" id="power" aria-labelledby="power-title">
  <div class="container">
    <div class="power__grid">
      <div class="power__copy" data-reveal>
        <p class="eyebrow">Live power flow</p>
        <h2 class="h2" id="power-title">Watch the power flow.</h2>
        <p class="lead">
          Power comes in from the adapter, goes in or out of the battery, and is used by your Mac. MacPUMP draws all
          three as one diagram, so you can see at a glance where every watt is going.
        </p>
        <ul class="check-list power__list">
          <li><strong>Updates about once a second</strong> from your Mac’s own power sensors.</li>
          <li>
            <strong>Always adds up.</strong> Your Mac’s power use is the adapter’s power minus what goes into the battery,
            so the diagram balances every time.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Changes with your battery.</strong> The battery is a source while it drains, a destination while it
            charges, and disappears when it is idle.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Time left if you unplug,</strong> from the energy left in the battery and your average power use over
            the last minute.
          </li>
        </ul>

        <details class="disclosure">
          <summary>For the technically curious</summary>
          <div class="disclosure__body">
            <p>
              MacPUMP reads adapter power and battery current and voltage from the SMC (<code>PDTR</code>,
              <code>B0AC</code>, <code>B0AV</code>) about once a second, and charge state from
              <code>IOPowerSources</code>. Slow-moving facts such as health, cycle count and pack capacity come from
              <code>AppleSmartBattery</code>, which only republishes about once a minute.
            </p>
            <p>
              The Mac’s load is derived, never read as a third sensor:
              <code>systemLoad = adapterPower − batteryPower</code> (battery positive while charging). Runtime uses the
              battery’s <em>nominal</em> voltage, because the full-charge voltage would overstate it by about 15%.
            </p>
            <p>
              Everything is read-only: the SMC is opened for reading and no key is ever written. Intel Macs fall back to
              slower IORegistry figures that refresh about once a minute.
            </p>
          </div>
        </details>
      </div>

      <div class="power__demo" data-reveal style="--reveal-delay:80ms">
        <PowerFlowDemo />
      </div>
    </div>

  </div>
</section>

<style>
  .power__grid {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr) minmax(0, 1fr);
    gap: clamp(2.5rem, 1rem + 5vw, 5.5rem);
    align-items: start;
  }

  .power__copy .lead {
    margin-top: var(--s-4);
  }

  .power__list {
    margin-top: var(--s-6);
  }

  .power__demo {
    position: sticky;
    top: calc(var(--nav-h) + var(--s-6));
  }

  @media (max-width: 960px) {
    .power__grid {
      grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 1fr);
    }

    .power__demo {
      position: static;
    }
  }

</style>
